---
intent: >
  Refactor parseBrainAide from heading-based body extraction
  (`## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub`) to paired
  HTML-comment marker extraction (`<!-- aide-prose-start -->` …
  `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`, etc.). Replace heading-walking with
  marker-pair detection over a closed lowercase grammar; surface
  every marker-layout violation class as `malformed-body` naming
  the offending marker; silently ignore bytes outside any pair;
  rename the parsed-result fields `playbookHub` → `playbook` and
  `researchHub` → `research` (in `src/types/index.ts` and every
  consumer/test); replace heading-based fixtures with marker-based
  fixtures and add tests for each violation class, the bytes-
  outside-pairs case, and the pre-pivot heading-only migration
  rejection; sweep this module's TypeScript for forbidden-vocabulary
  drift (`hub`, `vault`, `obsidian` outside the three carve-outs,
  `note` as synonym, `wikilink`, `hub note`); update orchestrator
  JSDoc and step-by-step inline narration to reflect the marker
  schema.
---

## Project Structure

This is a refactor of an existing module. The orchestrator's external
surface keeps the same names and arities — `parseBrainAide` (default
export), `parseBrainAideFromString`, and `interpolateArgs` retain
their signatures. What changes:

- Body extraction switches from heading walking to marker-pair walking.
- The `ok` variant fields rename: `playbookHub` → `playbook`,
  `researchHub` → `research` (the `prose` field name does not change).
- The body-extraction helper renames from `extractBodySections` to
  `extractMarkerSections` to track the new grammar.
- The `REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS` module-level constant retires; the
  marker grammar replaces it with a `MARKER_PAIRS` constant carrying
  the six exact marker tokens grouped by section in required order.
- Test fixtures replace heading-based bodies with marker-based bodies;
  vocabulary purge scrubs the test file's prose of `vault`, `obsidian`,
  `wikilink`, and the `hub` field-name suffix.

```
src/service/parseBrainAide/
├── .aide                         ← [existing, aligned] intent spec —
│                                    rewritten for the marker-pair body
├── plan.aide                     ← [this file, REPLACES the prior 5/5-done plan]
├── todo.aide                     ← [existing] alignment record (untouched)
├── index.ts                      ← [edit] orchestrator + helpers
│                                    exports unchanged in name and signature:
│                                      default async function parseBrainAide(root: string): Promise<ParseBrainAideResult>
│                                      export function parseBrainAideFromString(content: string): ParseBrainAideResult
│                                      export function interpolateArgs(config: BrainAideConfig): string[]
│                                    private helper renames:
│                                      function extractBodySections   →   function extractMarkerSections
│                                      signature:
│                                        function extractMarkerSections(body: string):
│                                          | { kind: "ok"; prose: string; playbook: string; research: string }
│                                          | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }
│                                    behavior changes:
│                                      - body extraction walks paired HTML-comment
│                                        markers (six exact byte sequences, lowercase,
│                                        case-sensitive, in fixed prose-then-playbook-
│                                        then-research order)
│                                      - body grammar is closed: every marker-layout
│                                        violation class (missing pair, malformed/typo'd
│                                        marker, unmatched closer, unmatched opener,
│                                        wrong section order, nested markers) returns
│                                        malformed-body naming the offending marker
│                                      - bytes outside any marker pair are silently
│                                        ignored
│                                      - the `ok` result carries `prose`, `playbook`,
│                                        and `research` (renamed from `playbookHub`/
│                                        `researchHub`); all three return verbatim
│                                        (byte-identical between an opener and its
│                                        matching closer, no trimming)
└── index.test.ts                 ← [edit] fixtures rebuilt with marker pairs;
                                     new test groups for each violation class,
                                     for bytes-outside-pairs silent-ignore,
                                     for the pre-pivot heading-based migration
                                     rejection, and for the renamed-field
                                     assertions; vocabulary purge scrubs
                                     `vault`/`obsidian`/`wikilink`/`note`
                                     drift from prose and fixture content
```

External edit in another file (called out for the implementor; the
`src/types/` change must land in the same step that switches the
parser to markers because the parser's `ok` result references the
central type):

```
src/types/index.ts                ← [edit] rename ParseBrainAideResult.ok
                                     fields `playbookHub` → `playbook`
                                     and `researchHub` → `research`.
                                     Update the union's JSDoc to describe
                                     the marker-pair body grammar (six
                                     exact marker tokens, lowercase, case-
                                     sensitive, in required order) and
                                     enumerate the marker-layout violation
                                     classes that surface as malformed-body.
                                     `BrainAideConfig` and
                                     `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` are
                                     unchanged.
```

Single-helper file layout from prior cycles still applies:
`extractMarkerSections`, `interpolateArgs`, and the
`parseBrainAideFromString` / `parseBrainAide` pair fit comfortably in
one `index.ts`. `extractMarkerSections` and `interpolateArgs` remain
defined ABOVE the default export per the function-ordering rule. No
new sub-helper folder.

## Prerequisites

This plan has no external module prerequisites. Every type and constant
it touches lives in files this plan edits directly
(`src/service/parseBrainAide/` and `src/types/index.ts`). The intent
spec at `src/service/parseBrainAide/.aide` is already aligned to the
marker-pair schema.

**Downstream-breakage notice (not a prerequisite — informational):**

- Renaming `playbookHub` → `playbook` and `researchHub` → `research` on
  the `ok` variant is a breaking change for consumers reading those
  fields. The known consumers per the spec's "per-consumer section
  ownership" outcome:
  - `provisionBrain` reads the playbook and research bodies — will fail
    to compile until updated. Governed by its own `plan.aide`.
  - The brain tool (`src/tools/brain/`) reads `prose` only — no change
    required.
  - `cli/sync` reads frontmatter only — no change required.
  - `buildBrainState` reads frontmatter only — no change required.
  Tightening the body grammar to require markers WILL also break any
  existing on-disk `brain.aide` that still uses the heading-based body
  (no marker pairs present). Those files now return `malformed-body`
  naming all six missing markers. That behavior is the spec's
  load-bearing strict-failure migration policy.

The downstream consumer that actually reads the renamed fields
(`provisionBrain`) is governed by its own `plan.aide` and depends on
this plan landing first.

## Plan

### 1. Rename `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` body fields and update the union JSDoc

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/type-system`, `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [x] Edit `src/types/index.ts`. In `ParseBrainAideResult`, rename two
      fields on the `ok` variant: `playbookHub` → `playbook`, and
      `researchHub` → `research`. The `prose` field name does not
      change. After this edit, the union shape is:

      ```
      | { kind: "ok"; config: BrainAideConfig; prose: string;
          playbook: string; research: string }
      | { kind: "missing" }
      | { kind: "malformed-frontmatter"; reason: string }
      | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }
      ```

      Keep `BrainAideConfig` and `BrainAideMcpServerConfig` unchanged —
      frontmatter shape is not part of this refactor.

      Rewrite the union's JSDoc to describe the marker-pair body grammar
      and enumerate the marker-layout violation classes:

      - On `"ok"`: each body field carries the verbatim bytes between
        its opening marker and its matching closing marker. The three
        recognized marker pairs, in required order, are:
        - `<!-- aide-prose-start -->` / `<!-- aide-prose-end -->`
        - `<!-- aide-playbook-start -->` / `<!-- aide-playbook-end -->`
        - `<!-- aide-research-start -->` / `<!-- aide-research-end -->`
        Marker tokens are lowercase, case-sensitive, with the literal
        shape `<!-- <token>-start -->` / `<!-- <token>-end -->` exactly.
      - On `"malformed-body"`: the body fails the closed marker-pair
        grammar. Document each violation class with an example reason
        format (the implementor cross-references the format strings
        from the helper in step 2):
        - missing pair → `"missing markers: <opener>, <closer>"`
          (lists every absent marker, in fixed prose-then-playbook-
          then-research scan order).
        - malformed/typo'd marker token (any deviation from the literal
          shape — uppercase, mixed-case, missing internal spaces,
          extra internal whitespace) → `"unknown marker: <as-written>"`.
        - closing marker without a prior matching opener →
          `"unmatched closing marker: <closer> appeared without a
          prior <matching-opener>"`.
        - opening marker without a closer →
          `"unmatched opening marker: <opener> has no matching
          <expected-closer>"`.
        - wrong section order →
          `"marker order violation: <out-of-order-opener> appeared
          before <expected-prior-opener>"`.
        - nested markers (any marker found inside another pair's
          opener-closer span) →
          `"nested marker: <inner-opener> appeared inside the
          <outer-token> section"`.

      Confirm by grep that no consumer in `src/` references the old
      field names `playbookHub`/`researchHub` outside the parser module
      itself; all such references will be repointed by step 2 and step 3
      (and the `provisionBrain` plan picks up its own consumer-side
      rename in its own plan, not this one).

### 2. Replace the heading walker with a marker-pair walker, rename the helper, propagate the field rename through the orchestrator

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`, `coding-playbook/foundations/anti-patterns`

- [x] 2a. Edit `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`. Retire the
      `REQUIRED_BODY_HEADINGS` module-level constant and the
      `extractBodySections` helper. Introduce a new module-level
      constant `MARKER_PAIRS` declared above all helpers, capturing the
      three marker pairs in required order:

      ```
      const MARKER_PAIRS = [
        { token: "prose",    open: "<!-- aide-prose-start -->",    close: "<!-- aide-prose-end -->" },
        { token: "playbook", open: "<!-- aide-playbook-start -->", close: "<!-- aide-playbook-end -->" },
        { token: "research", open: "<!-- aide-research-start -->", close: "<!-- aide-research-end -->" },
      ] as const;
      ```

      `as const` preserves the literal types so `token`'s union narrows
      to `"prose" | "playbook" | "research"`. The three `token` values
      are also the destination field names on the `ok` result — the
      user-facing marker base name and the typed-object key are
      visibly the same word.

- [x] 2b. Add a new private helper `extractMarkerSections` declared
      ABOVE `interpolateArgs` and the default export per the
      function-ordering rule. Signature:

      ```
      function extractMarkerSections(body: string):
        | { kind: "ok"; prose: string; playbook: string; research: string }
        | { kind: "malformed-body"; reason: string }
      ```

      Behavior — declarative pipeline the implementor walks once
      through the body. Each step short-circuits to a typed
      `malformed-body` reason. The implementor does NOT reuse the
      heading-regex algorithm; the marker grammar is a different
      shape (paired tokens, not single-line headings).

      - **Locate every recognized marker.** Scan the body for every
        occurrence of any of the six exact byte sequences in
        `MARKER_PAIRS` (three openers + three closers). Use literal
        substring matching, not regex with character classes — the
        grammar is closed and case-sensitive, so the cheapest and
        most auditable check is `body.indexOf(<exact-token>)`
        repeated, or a single linear scan that matches each candidate
        position against the literal byte sequences. Build an ordered
        list of `{ tokenKind: "open"|"close"; sectionToken:
        "prose"|"playbook"|"research"; index: number }` entries in
        document order.

      - **Detect malformed/typo'd marker tokens.** Beyond the six
        recognized sequences, scan for any HTML-comment that LOOKS
        like a marker but is not one of the six exact bytes — i.e.
        any `<!--…aide…-->` substring whose contents are not exactly
        the lowercase recognized form. The detection set the
        implementor must catch, drawn from the spec's "Bad examples"
        and "undesired outcomes" lists:
        - uppercase variants (e.g. `<!-- AIDE-PROSE-START -->`)
        - mixed-case variants (e.g. `<!-- Aide-prose-start -->`)
        - no surrounding spaces (e.g. `<!--aide-prose-start-->`)
        - extra internal whitespace (e.g. `<!-- aide-prose-start  -->`,
          `<!--  aide-prose-start -->`)
        - typos (e.g. `<!-- aide-prose-strart -->`,
          `<!-- aide-playboook-end -->`)
        - missing the `aide-` prefix (e.g. `<!-- prose-start -->`)
        Any such occurrence returns
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "unknown marker: <as-
        written>" }` naming the FIRST malformed marker encountered
        left-to-right. Use a permissive HTML-comment regex (e.g.
        `/<!--[^>]*aide[^>]*-->/gi`) to enumerate candidate marker-
        like spans, then filter out the six exact recognized
        sequences; what remains is the malformed set, and the first
        one in document order is the reason. Document this strategy
        in an inline comment per the orchestrator inline-narration
        rule (this is non-obvious detail, not just narration).

      - **Required-pair presence check.** If any of the six
        recognized markers is absent, return
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "missing markers:
        <comma-separated list of every absent marker, in fixed
        prose-then-playbook-then-research order: open of section N
        before close of section N>" }`. List every missing marker
        in one response so the user fixes them all in one edit
        (matches the spec's good example for the pre-pivot heading-
        based file: all six missing markers listed). Format examples:
        - one missing pair (research only):
          `"missing markers: <!-- aide-research-start -->,
          <!-- aide-research-end -->"`
        - all six missing (pre-pivot heading-based file):
          `"missing markers: <!-- aide-prose-start -->,
          <!-- aide-prose-end -->, <!-- aide-playbook-start -->,
          <!-- aide-playbook-end -->, <!-- aide-research-start -->,
          <!-- aide-research-end -->"`

      - **Unmatched-closer check.** Walking the marker list in
        document order, if a `close` marker for some section
        appears before its matching `open` marker, return
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "unmatched closing
        marker: <closer> appeared without a prior <matching-
        opener>" }`. The reason names the offending closer and
        the opener it would have paired with.

      - **Unmatched-opener check.** Walking in document order, if
        an `open` marker has no later matching `close` (the section
        runs to end-of-file with no closer), return
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "unmatched opening
        marker: <opener> has no matching <expected-closer>" }`.
        The parser does NOT silently treat end-of-file as an
        implicit closer (per the spec's bad example calling out
        exactly that recovery).

      - **Section-order check.** Walking in document order, if the
        `open` markers do not appear in prose-then-playbook-then-
        research order, return
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "marker order violation:
        <out-of-order-opener> appeared before <expected-prior-
        opener>" }`. The required order is fixed: prose first,
        playbook second, research third. No exceptions, no auto-
        reorder. Note: this check fires only after presence and
        matching pass — if a section's pair is missing, the
        missing-markers reason fires first.

      - **Nesting check.** For each matched pair `(open_i, close_i)`,
        if any other recognized marker (open or close, of any
        section) appears at a byte offset strictly between
        `open_i.index + open_i.length` and `close_i.index`, return
        `{ kind: "malformed-body", reason: "nested marker:
        <inner-token> appeared inside the <outer-section-token>
        section" }`. The three sections are siblings, never parent-
        child. Spec's "Bad examples" calls out this exact failure
        mode — a forgiving stack-based grammar would silently accept
        the nesting; the parser must not.

      - **Section slicing.** When all checks pass, slice each
        section as the bytes between
        `open.index + open.length` and `close.index`. The slice is
        byte-identical to what the user wrote between those bounds;
        no trimming, no normalization, no leading-newline strip
        (the prior heading-based extractor's strip-one-newline
        backward-compat carve-out goes away with the heading shape;
        marker boundaries are bytes, not lines, so there is no
        analogous behavior to preserve).

      - **Bytes outside any pair are silently ignored by
        construction.** The slicer never reads the regions between
        `header-end` and `prose-open`, between `prose-close` and
        `playbook-open`, between `playbook-close` and `research-
        open`, or between `research-close` and end-of-file. The
        parser's job is to extract the three named sections, not
        to police the rest of the file.

      Add a JSDoc block above the helper summarizing the pipeline in
      the order above (presence → match → order → nesting → slice).
      JSDoc names the helper's role: "single closed-grammar walker
      over the body's marker pairs; every marker-layout violation
      class returns malformed-body naming the offending marker."

- [x] 2c. Update `parseBrainAideFromString`. The function's pipeline
      stays the same (frontmatter parse → required fields → deprecated
      fields → body extraction → ok); only the body-extraction step
      and the `ok` field names change. After successful frontmatter
      validation:

      - Call `extractMarkerSections(body)` instead of
        `extractBodySections(body)`.
      - On `malformed-body`, return that result directly.
      - On `ok`, return
        `{ kind: "ok", config, prose, playbook, research }` —
        destructure the helper's return into the renamed fields.

      Update the function's JSDoc to describe the marker-pair body
      grammar (replacing the old heading-based description). The
      "Validation order" block keeps the same three-step shape; only
      step 3's prose changes to "Body grammar — the closed-vocabulary
      marker walker (`extractMarkerSections`) checks that every
      required marker pair is present and well-formed; every
      marker-layout violation class returns `malformed-body` naming
      the offending marker."

      Update the inline step-by-step comments inside
      `parseBrainAideFromString` (per the progressive-disclosure
      tier-3 contract for orchestrators). The comments currently
      say "Step 5: Extract the three required named body sections
      via the closed-vocabulary walker. Unknown headings and
      missing required sections both surface as malformed-body."
      Rewrite to describe the marker walker and the violation
      classes it surfaces.

- [x] 2d. Update `parseBrainAide` (the default export) JSDoc. The
      "Result branches" block currently says `"ok"` requires the
      three `## Prose` / `## Playbook hub` / `## Research hub`
      sections; rewrite to describe the marker pairs.
      "Load-bearing invariants" block: rename `playbookHub` →
      `playbook` and `researchHub` → `research` in the prose
      describing which fields are returned verbatim.

- [x] 2e. Vocabulary sweep across `index.ts`. Confirm no remaining
      occurrences of forbidden tokens in identifiers, JSDoc, inline
      comments, or string constants:

      - `hub` (anywhere — field names, comments, string constants,
        JSDoc) — should be zero occurrences after this step. The
        retired heading strings `## Playbook hub` and `## Research
        hub` go away entirely.
      - `vault` — zero occurrences; the parser describes its body
        in terms of `prose` / `playbook` / `research` and the
        `brain.aide` file path.
      - `obsidian` — zero occurrences. Note the carve-out: the spec's
        forbidden-token list applies to identifiers, comments, and
        string constants in this module; user-facing example fixture
        bytes that travel through the parser as test fixtures are
        not part of the parser module's source vocabulary.
      - `note` used as a synonym for "file" — zero occurrences.
      - `wikilink` — zero occurrences.
      - `hub note` — zero occurrences.

      Use the spec's anti-example carve-out exactly once if needed:
      naming a forbidden token inside a JSDoc that explicitly
      forbids it (no occurrence is currently expected).

