### 4. Verify the parser in isolation, then surface downstream breakage

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [x] 4a. Run the parser-module test suite (e.g.
      `npm test -- parseBrainAide` or the equivalent vitest
      filter scoped to this module's test file). Every test in
      `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts` must pass. If
      any fails, fix the parser code (not the test) — the spec
      is the contract; the tests assert the contract; failing
      tests mean the implementation drifted from the spec. Do
      NOT relax assertions to make tests pass.

      Likely failure modes:
      - The marker-pair walker mishandles end-of-file when the
        research pair is the last content in the file with no
        trailing newline. Fix: the slice upper bound for any
        section is the close marker's `index`, NEVER `body.length`
        unless the close marker is missing (in which case the
        unmatched-opener check should have fired earlier — if
        it did not, the check is wrong).
      - The malformed-marker scan misses a case because the
        permissive HTML-comment regex is too narrow. Tighten the
        regex to match any `<!--…-->` whose contents contain
        `aide` (case-insensitive on the substring check, then
        case-sensitive on the literal-form filter).
      - The nesting check fires false positives when a recognized
        marker appears in body text outside any pair (e.g. the
        bytes-outside-pairs-with-marker-shaped-content edge case
        in step 3j). Fix: the nesting check considers only
        markers that are PART of a recognized matched pair; the
        bytes-outside scan is independent.
      - A test asserting on a literal reason string fails because
        the helper's reason-format string drifts from the type's
        JSDoc enumeration. Fix: pick one canonical string format
        per violation class, document it in the helper's JSDoc
        AND in the type's JSDoc, and write the tests to that
        format. Drift between helper and type docs is itself a
        bug to fix in this step.

- [x] 4b. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` from the project root. The
      parser's own files must compile cleanly. Downstream
      consumers (`provisionBrain`, `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`,
      and any consumer that read the renamed fields) WILL fail to
      compile because they reference the old `playbookHub` /
      `researchHub` field names. Record the list of failing files
      in the implementor's hand-off message; do NOT modify those
      files. They are governed by their own `plan.aide` files (the
      orchestrator runs them next in the dependency chain).

      The only files inside this plan's scope that may surface
      `tsc` errors are `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`,
      `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.test.ts`, and
      `src/types/index.ts`. All three must compile; if not, the
      type rename in step 1, the helper rewrite in step 2, or the
      test rewrites in step 3 need another pass.

## Decisions

- **Marker detection is literal-substring + permissive-comment
  rejection, not a single grammar walk.** The closed lowercase
  case-sensitive grammar admits exactly six byte sequences as
  recognized markers; everything else that looks like an HTML
  comment containing "aide" is malformed. A naive single-pass
  state machine would conflate "this is a recognized marker" and
  "this is content" — and would silently miss the
  uppercase/mixed-case/typo cases the spec explicitly rules out
  in its "Bad examples". The two-phase approach (recognize-or-
  reject, then walk recognized markers for layout violations) is
  the cheapest shape that catches every violation class the spec
  enumerates without adding a stack-based parser. Keep both phases
  in the same `extractMarkerSections` helper — they share state
  and a single body string, and splitting them would force two
  passes over the same bytes for no clarity gain.

- **Reason-format strings are documented in two places: the
  helper's JSDoc and the union's JSDoc.** Tests assert literal
  reason strings, and a drift between the helper and the type
  declaration would surface as confusing test failures. The
  helper's JSDoc is the source of truth (it produces the strings);
  the type's JSDoc mirrors it for consumers reading the central
  type. Both documents update together — a future change to one
  reason format requires the same change in both places. The
  format strings themselves come from the spec's "Good examples"
  block, which uses the canonical phrasings (`"missing markers:
  …"`, `"unknown marker: …"`, `"unmatched closing marker: …
  appeared without a prior …"`, `"unmatched opening marker: …
  has no matching …"`, `"marker order violation: … appeared
  before …"`, `"nested marker: … appeared inside the … section"`).

- **Field rename `playbookHub` → `playbook` and `researchHub` →
  `research` propagates through `src/types/index.ts`, the parser
  module, and every test in this module. The `prose` field name
  is unchanged.** The retired `hub` suffix is the spec's outcome
  #17 carve-out — `playbookHub` and `researchHub` are explicitly
  named in the parser spec's "undesired" block as "the same
  regression in different clothing" relative to the broader
  vocabulary purge. The storage-agnostic field names are exactly
  the marker token base names: `prose`, `playbook`, `research`.
  Downstream consumers that read these fields (`provisionBrain`)
  fail to compile after the rename and pick up the rename in
  their own `plan.aide`. The brain tool, sync, and buildBrainState
  do not touch the renamed fields and are unaffected.

- **`MARKER_PAIRS` is a module-level `as const` constant
  carrying open/close pairs together.** The walker iterates it for
  every check (presence, order, nesting); the missing-marker
  reason builder iterates it; the type's JSDoc references it as
  the canonical grammar source. A future change (a new pair, a
  marker rename, a tightening of the grammar) is a one-line edit
  to this constant. The `as const` keyword preserves the literal
  string and discriminant types, so the section-token field
  narrows to `"prose" | "playbook" | "research"` and TypeScript
  catches a typo in the destructure of the helper's return.

- **Bytes outside any marker pair are silently ignored, INCLUDING
  marker-shaped content.** The spec's outcome #6 is unambiguous:
  notes-to-self, scratch text, comments outside the recognized
  pairs are not the parser's concern. The malformed-marker scan
  consequently must be bounded — it scans only positions that
  are NOT inside any recognized matched pair, and it reports a
  malformed marker only when the marker-shaped content is in a
  position where a recognized marker would be valid. This
  decision is the parser's concern boundary: the parser extracts
  three named sections; it does not police the rest of the file.
  A user who legitimately writes `<!-- aide-misc -->` in a
  scratch region between two pairs gets a clean parse.

- **Section-order check fires only after presence and matching
  pass.** A user who has written all six markers but in the
  wrong order returns `marker order violation`; a user who has
  also forgotten one pair returns `missing markers` first. The
  order is: presence → match → order → nesting → slice. This
  matches the spec's "halts the pipeline; the parser does not
  attempt recovery" invariant — each check produces one
  malformed-body reason, and the user fixes that one issue and
  re-runs.

- **Nesting check considers only markers that are part of a
  recognized matched pair.** A stray opener or closer that is
  itself unmatched is caught by the unmatched-opener or
  unmatched-closer check; the nesting check is the LAST
  layout check before slicing because its precondition is
  "every recognized marker has a matched partner, and we know
  which spans they bound." Running it earlier would either
  duplicate the unmatched-marker checks or false-positive on
  bytes-outside-pairs marker-shaped content.

- **No new sub-helper folder for the marker walker.** The
  `extractMarkerSections` private helper plus the existing
  `parseBrainAideFromString` / `parseBrainAide` /
  `interpolateArgs` trio still fit comfortably under ~300 lines.
  The progressive-disclosure single-helper exception still
  applies. The marker walker is grammatically more involved
  than the heading walker it replaces, but it is one
  responsibility (extract three named sections from a closed
  marker grammar); splitting it across multiple sub-helpers
  would fragment the violation-detection state without making
  any single helper simpler.

- **No backward-compat strip on `prose`.** The heading-based
  extractor stripped a single leading newline from `prose` for
  test assertions written against the pre-widening shape. The
  marker-pair extractor has no analogous concern: marker
  boundaries are bytes, not lines, so the slicer reads exactly
  the bytes between `open.index + open.length` and
  `close.index`. Tests assert against those exact bytes via the
  rebuilt `extractCanonicalSection` helper, so there is no
  drift to absorb. This is a deliberate cleanup: the strip was
  a transitional carve-out that has no place in the closed
  marker grammar.

- **Field rename and grammar switch land atomically in one
  cycle.** Splitting the rename into one cycle and the grammar
  switch into another would force the parser to either (a)
  reference the old type with the new grammar, or (b) reference
  the new type with the old grammar — both states are broken
  and require coordinated fixes across consumers. Co-locating
  both changes here means the parser, its tests, and the
  central type land at the same shape. The widening is breaking
  for the field-name consumers (`provisionBrain`), and that
  breakage is intentional and tracked through the cascade
  in those modules' own plans.

- **Strict-failure migration is intentional and tested
  explicitly.** A pre-pivot heading-based `brain.aide` returns
  `malformed-body` naming all six missing markers. No silent
  default fills them in; no `aide_upgrade` carve-out auto-
  migrates. The user hand-edits their file to add the markers
  (or copies the new defaults from the bundled scaffold). This
  matches the spec's outcome #7 ("Strict-failure migration: a
  pre-pivot `brain.aide` using the old heading-based body
  schema returns `malformed-body` with a reason naming the
  missing markers") and prevents the package from authoring
  content the user is supposed to own. A dedicated test in
  step 3i asserts this behavior so a future refactor that
  introduces silent defaults will fail visibly.

- **The disk read path is unchanged.** `parseBrainAide(root)`
  still reads `.aide/config/brain.aide` — the path move from
  earlier cycles is preserved. This plan touches only the body
  grammar (heading → markers) and the body field names
  (`playbookHub` → `playbook`, `researchHub` → `research`).
  Sync, which reads frontmatter only, is unaffected by both.

- **Step 4b records but does not fix downstream breakage.** The
  downstream module that reads the renamed fields
  (`provisionBrain` and its `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` helper)
  has its own `plan.aide` for the marker-grammar cascade. Fixing
  it inside this plan would expand its scope past the single-
  module rule. The verify step's job is to make the breakage
  visible — the orchestrator picks up that plan next.
